  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nCurrently, upper-level shortwave troughing is centered to the \nnorthwest with strong ridging over the desert southwest. The Bermuda \nHigh is also strong to our east. This will promote continuation of a \nsouthwesterly flow pattern with moisture and vertical ascent \nsufficient for diurnal rain chances. This morning, cloud cover has \nlimited heating with the higher terrain being the focus again for \nbetter coverage. PWATs remain near 1.7 to 1.9 with peak instability \naround 1,500 J/kg. As in recent days, isolated flooding is possible \nwithin slower-moving or training storms. Strong wind gusts are also \npossible. Saturday's pattern will be very similar with a slight \nincrease in upper-level flow to over 40 kts and potentially less \nmoisture. This will keep chances for scattered showers and storms, \nespecially along the higher terrain. By Sunday, the pattern becomes \nmore zonal aloft but with better low-level heating. Overall, \ninstability and moisture will be comparable to recent days. By \nMonday, flow aloft will strengthen again with further heating in the \nlower-levels increasing overall lapse rates to 6 C/km or greater. \nHowever, the environment will be inhibited by drier air arriving \nfrom the north. \n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, the pattern will become more northwesterly \nwith similarly elevated mid-level lapse rates, followed by an \nincrease in moisture. This will lead to better potential of \nconvection compared to Monday, likely stronger than what is expected \nearlier in the period. Wednesday into Thursday, the upper jet will \nprogress southward, leading to better support and at least some \nshear that could help organize storms.",
